/**
 * @page data_logging Logging information
 * 
 * @if DOX_NAV
 * @ref guide_book_top "Go back to the guide book topics page"
 * @endif
 * 
 * @section lib_logger Library log handler
 * 
 * By default, the library uses a simple log handler, which outputs the logged messages to the command line/terminal window. If the terminal window supports colored fonts, warnings are
 * displayed in a yellow font, errors in a red font, and any other log messages in a white font.
 * 
 * @subsection lib_log_funcs Logging functions
 * 
 * Information logging functions are not exclusive only to the library. API list provides a full @ref logging "list of logging functions" that can be used in the user program to log 
 * important information using the same library log handler if necessary.
 * 
 * The logging functions support the following @ref lime::LogLevel "log levels":
 * <ul>
 *    <li>Critical</li>
 *    <li>Error</li>
 *    <li>Warning</li>
 *    <li>Info</li>
 *    <li>Verbose</li>
 *    <li>Debug</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * Logging functions are overloaded and support formatted C-style string messages (use of format specifiers, for example ``"%.8f"``, and so on) and C++ style string messages. 
 * 
 * @section custom_logger Custom log handler
 * 
 * If the default library log handler is not sufficient, a custom log handler function can be defined and can be used to override the default log handler and its behaviour. To register a custom log 
 * handler use ``registerLogHandler(...)`` function and pass the address of the new log handler function as an argument. Since log handler registration functions are overloaded, it is possible to
 * register a log handler that can handle either C or C++ style strings. Reference to the log handler registration functions:
 * <ul>
 *    <li> @ref lime::registerLogHandler(const lime::LogHandlerCString) "registerLogHandler(const LogHandlerCString handler)" </li>
 *    <li> @ref lime::registerLogHandler(const lime::LogHandler) "registerLogHandler(const LogHandler handler)" </li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * <b>Register only a single log handler that operates using your preferred style of strings.</b> Support for C and C++ string style message logging is maintained despite the preferred log handler 
 * string style.
 * 
 * @subsection custom_logger_example Example code of a custom logger
 * 
 * Example code registers a custom log handler, that logs messages to a ``deviceLogs.txt`` file instead of terminal/command line window. Custom log handler logs C++ style string messages. However,
 * the logging of C style string messages is also supported despite the preferred log handler string style. Important to note, that the custom log handler does not take the log level into consideration.
 * 
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <iostream>
 * #include <vector>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceRegistry.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDevice.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceHandle.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/ToString.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDescriptor.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/Logger.h>
 * #include <fstream>
 * #include <string>
 * 
 * using namespace std::literals::string_literals;
 * 
 * static std::string logFileName = "deviceLogs.txt";
 * 
 * void myLogHandler(const lime::LogLevel level, const std::string& message)
 * {
 *    std::ofstream logFile(logFileName.c_str(), std::ios_base::app);
 *    logFile << message << std::endl;
 *    logFile.close();
 * }
 * 
 * int main()
 * {
 *    lime::registerLogHandler(myLogHandler);
 *    std::vector<lime::DeviceHandle> listOfDevices = lime::DeviceRegistry::enumerate();
 *    if(listOfDevices.empty())
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "No devices detected!\n";
 *       return 0;
 *    }
 * 
 *    lime::SDRDevice * device = lime::DeviceRegistry::makeDevice(listOfDevices.front());
 *    if(device == nullptr)
 *    {
 *       std::cout << "Failed to connect to SDR device\n";
 *       return 1;
 *    }
 * 
 *    uint8_t CLK_CGEN = 3;
 *    uint8_t ChA = 0;
 *    lime::SDRDescriptor deviceDesc = device->GetDescriptor();
 *    lime::info("Logging information for "s + deviceDesc.name + " device:"s);
 *    lime::info("Core clock frequency = %.6f Hz", device->GetClockFreq(CLK_CGEN, ChA)); // We can still use formatted C style strings even if the handler only supports C++ style strings
 *    lime::warning("End of program! Deallocating device resources!");
 *    lime::DeviceRegistry::freeDevice(device);
 *    return 0;
 * }
 * @endcode
 * 
 * Output of the example code shows that in addition to our log messages, library logs are also present:
 * 
 * @image{inline} html custom_logger_output.png
 * 
 */